      "text": "��<!DOCTYPE html PUBLIC \"-//W3C//DTD XHTML 1.0 Transitional//EN\" \"http://www.w3.org/TR/xhtml1/DTD/xhtml1-transitional.dtd\"><html><body><p><p>There are two main types of roles.</p>\n<p><b>Ascribed:�</b>�a role that is given to you automatically, with no effort on your part.�</p><p><i>For example: Queen Elizabeth II has the role of Queen of England because it was conferred upon her by inheritance.�</i></p><p><i>Another example: the moment you are born you are given the role of� \"child\" to your parents.</i></p>\n<p><b>Achieved: </b>a role that is acquired through hard work, ambition and merit.��</p><p><i>For example: the role of Prime Minister.</i></p>\n<p></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\"><img src=\"..\\image_chapter_15859.jpeg\" alt=\"Image result for Achieved Roles\"><br></p>\n<p>In modern societies, achieved roles are usually seen as more important because the most capable and talented people can be put in a position they are best suited for to make positive choices for a societies future success.</p>\n<h5><b>Learning Achieved Roles</b></h5>\n<p>Achieved roles are learned \u0013  how?</p>\n<p>Socialization during childhood is a starting point to learning roles.� We develop clear ideas of what is appropriate behavior.� This starts within our families and later, we understand the expectations of our society as a whole.� These ideas of behavior become ingrained within us and. Over time, we accept these rules as truths.</p>\n<table border=\"0\" align=\"center\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><span style=\"font-size: large; color: #339966;\">Sources of Knowledge About Roles</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: large; color: #339966;\">- Parents������������ - Family</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: large; color: #339966;\">- Playmates�������� - Educators</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: large; color: #339966;\">- Friends������������ - Church</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: large; color: #339966;\">- Mass Media</span></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p></p>\n<p></p>\n<p>Identifying and imitating roles is simple and straight forward.��If a role is shown in a favorable way, then the appeal will be high for the role and a person will imitate the role.<i>��</i></p><p><i>For example: if a child sees their parent hold and cuddle a baby and provide it care, the child may copy the behavior with a doll.��Later, they may extend this role by caring for younger siblings.</i></p></p></body></html>",
